465 | #############################################################################
466 | #
467 | #  Security, authentication and authorization
468 | #
469 | 
470 | #  JSPWiki supports a plugin-based interface for talking to different
471 | #  kinds of authentication and authorization systems. By "authentication,"
472 | #  we mean a system for logging in a user to establish their identity.
473 | #  By "authorization," we mean a system for figuring out what actions
474 | #  users can perform based on their authenticated identities.
475 | #
476 | #  For users looking to get started quickly, the default settings below
477 | #  should work fine. In addition to the properties below, you may also
478 | #  want to modify the security policy file WEB-INF/jspwiki.policy. See
479 | #  the policy file for more details.
480 | #
481 | #  AUTHENTICATION
482 | #
483 | #  For authentication, JSPWiki uses JAAS (Java Authentication and Authorization
484 | #  Service) in combination with a servlet filter that picks up any credentials
485 | #  set by the servlet container. The Authentication system is configured below.
486 | #
487 | #  You must choose either (A) Container or (B) Custom authentication. (B) is the default.
488 | #
489 | #  A) CONTAINER AUTHENTICATION
490 | #  JSPWiki will always (passively) collect credentials supplied by your servlet
491 | #  container, via HttpServletRequest.getUserPrincipal/getRemote user. You do not
492 | #  need to do anything to enable this. In addition, you can cause JSPWiki users
493 | #  to log in to the web container by uncommenting the the <security-constraint>
494 | #  elements in WEB-INF/web.xml.
495 | #
496 | #  B) CUSTOM AUTHENTICATION
497 | #  If you do not wish to use container-managed authentication, you can use JSPWiki's
498 | #  own custom authentication system. This uses a JAAS LoginModule (supplied below)
499 | #  to log in the user. You can use any JAAS LoginModule you want.
500 | #  The default class is org.apache.wiki.auth.login.UserDatabaseLoginModule,
501 | #  which compares the supplied username and hashed password with the values stored
502 | #  in the configured UserDatabase (see USER DATABASE below).
503 | #
504 | #  Supply the JAAS LoginModule class used for custom authentication here.
505 | #  The implementation MUST have a zero-argument constructor (as noted in the
506 | #  javax.security.auth.spi.LoginModule Javadocs).
507 | jspwiki.loginModule.class = org.apache.wiki.auth.login.UserDatabaseLoginModule
508 | 
509 | #
510 | # JAAS LoginContext parameters used to initialize the LoginModule. Note that 'param1'
511 | #  etc. should be replaced with the actual parameter names. The parameter names and
512 | # values will be loaded to a Map and passed to the LoginModule as the 'options' parameter
513 | # when its initialize() method is called. The default UserDatabaseLoginModule class does
514 | # not need any options.
515 | #jspwiki.loginModule.options.param1 = value1
516 | #jspwiki.loginModule.options.param2 = value2
517 | 
518 | #
519 | # Login Throttling - Whether logins should be throttled to limit bruce-force attempts.
520 | #
521 | jspwiki.login.throttling=true
522 | 
523 | #
524 | #  Cookie authentication & assertion
525 | #
526 | #  If this value is set to "true", then JSPWiki will allow you to "assert" an
527 | #  identity using a cookie.  It's still considered to be unsafe, just like no
528 | #  login at all, but it is useful when you have no need to force everyone to login.
529 | #
530 | #  By default, this is on.
531 | #
532 | #jspwiki.cookieAssertions=true
533 | 
534 | #
535 | #  If you would like to keep your users logged in for weeks at a time, you can
536 | #  turn on "cookie authentication" feature.  However, this comes with important
537 | #  security caveats:
538 | #  1) User will stay logged in into your system for weeks.  This means that if
539 | #     someone manages to nab the cookie during this time, they can pretend to
540 | #     be that user.
541 | #  2) The mappings between cookies and users are written in your filesystem,
542 | #     in $jspwiki.workDir/logincookies.  Access to this directory means that
543 | #     the ability to fake anyone in the wiki, so please make sure that only
544 | #     the proper admin has read access to this directory.
545 | #
546 | #  By default, cookie authentication is off.
547 | #
548 | #jspwiki.cookieAuthentication=false
549 | 
550 | #
551 | #  Defines how many days the cookies are kept, and how often the people have to log in.
552 | #  The default is two weeks, i.e. 14 days.  If you need a shorter period than one day,
553 | #  turn off cookie authentication, then tweak your web.xml to allow for longer sessions.
554 | #
555 | #jspwiki.cookieAuthentication.expiry=14
556 | 
557 | #
558 | #
559 | #  AUTHORIZATION
560 | #
561 | #  For authorization, JSPWiki has a two-tier system. When we want to
562 | #  determine whether a user has permission to perform a certain action,
563 | #  we first consult (A) an external "authorizer" to determine if the user
564 | #  is a member of the required role. In addition to checking its external
565 | #  authorizer, it also checks (B) its GroupManager for wiki-managed groups.
566 | #
567 | #  A) EXTERNAL AUTHORIZATION
568 | #  By default, JSPWiki uses the servlet container's authorization service
569 | #  for to check what roles the user belongs to (that is, it calls
570 | #  HttpServletRequest.isUserInRole(String)). After the user authenticates,
571 | #  the default Authorizer (WebContainerAuthorizer) checks to see if the user
572 | #  belongs to the roles listed in web.xml using <security-role>/<role-name> or
573 | #  <auth-constraint>/<role-name> elements. However, you can use another
574 | #  Authorizer if you wish; specify that class here.
575 | 
576 | jspwiki.authorizer = org.apache.wiki.auth.authorize.WebContainerAuthorizer
577 | 
578 | #  B) GROUPS
579 | #  As an additional source of authorization, users can belong to discretionary
580 | #  "wiki groups" that the users manage themselves. Wiki groups are stored in a
581 | #  GroupDatabase. The default group database uses an XML file for persistent
582 | #  storage. Override with your own GroupDatabase implementation with this property:
583 | 
584 | jspwiki.groupdatabase = org.apache.wiki.auth.authorize.XMLGroupDatabase
585 | 
586 | #  The default group database implementation stores member lists
587 | #  in an XML file. The location of this file should be in a secure directory
588 | #  in the filesystem; for example, in /etc or your servlet container's
589 | #  configuration directory If you do not supply a value for this property,
590 | #  a blank group database will be initialized in the WEB-INF/ directory of the
591 | #  deployed webapp. Since these directories are often overwritten when webapps
592 | #  are undeployed or redeployed, you should probably set this property to
593 | #  something useful as soon as you can. But for test wikis, it's probably
594 | #  ok to leave this un-set, as long as users know that their groups could
595 | #  "disappear" if the wiki app is ever redeployed.
596 | 
597 | #jspwiki.xmlGroupDatabaseFile = /etc/tomcat/groupdatabase.xml
598 | 
599 | #  USER DATABASE
600 | #  User's wiki profiles are stored in a UserDatabase. The default user database
601 | #  uses an XML file for persistent storage.
602 | #  Override with your own UserDatabase implementation with this property:
603 | 
604 | jspwiki.userdatabase = org.apache.wiki.auth.user.XMLUserDatabase
605 | 
606 | #  The default user database implementation stores usernames and passwords
607 | #  in an XML file. Passwords are SHA-1 hashed. The location of this file
608 | #  should be in a secure directory in the filesystem; for example, in
609 | #  /etc or your servlet container's configuration directory.
610 | #  If you do not supply a value for this property, a blank user database
611 | #  will be initialized in the WEB-INF/ directory of the deployed webapp.
612 | #  Since these directories are often overwritten when webapps are
613 | #  undeployed or redeployed, you should probably set this property to
614 | #  something useful as soon as you can. But for test wikis, it's probably
615 | #  ok to leave this un-set, as long as users know that their profiles could
616 | #  "disappear" if the wiki app is ever redeployed.
617 | 
618 | #jspwiki.xmlUserDatabaseFile = /etc/tomcat/userdatabase.xml
619 | 
620 | # You can also use a JDBC database for storing user profiles,
621 | # to be configured below in the JDBC section. See the online
622 | # AuthenticationAndAuthorization2.3 docs and the JavaDoc for
623 | # the JDBCUserDatabase class for details on how to configure it.
624 | #
625 | 
626 | #jspwiki.userdatabase = org.apache.wiki.auth.user.JDBCUserDatabase
627 | 
628 | #  If your JSPWiki user database shares login information with your
629 | #  web container's authentication realm, you can configure JSPWiki to
630 | #  add container users. At present, this only works with JDBCUserDatabase,
631 | #  and only if you've configured your web container to use a database
632 | #  with compatible columns and tables. If you don't know what this means,
633 | #  then leave this property set to FALSE (the default).
634 | 
635 | #jspwiki.userdatabase.isSharedWithContainer = false
636 | 
637 | #  ACCESS CONTROL LISTS
638 | #  Last but not least, JSPWiki needs a way of reading and persisting page
639 | #  access control lists. The default implementation reads these from the page
640 | #  markup. For example: "[{ALLOW edit Charlie}]". If using a custom
641 | #  ACL manager, specify the AclManager implementation class here:
642 | 
643 | jspwiki.aclManager = org.apache.wiki.auth.acl.DefaultAclManager
644 | 
