808 | #
809 | #  JavaMail configuration. If you wish to allow your users to recover
810 | #  their passwords via email, you should configure these properties.
811 | #  JavaMail can use either a container-managed JNDI resource factory
812 | #  (recommended, and the default), or a stand-alone factory whose properties
813 | #  are configured with mail.* properties in this file (below).
814 | #
815 | 
816 | #  A. Configure the address from which the email appears to come.
817 | #     If you're going to use a mail session obtained via JNDI, this setting
818 | #     will only be used if it hasn't already been configured in the obtained
819 | #     session itself. If you comment it out, JSPWiki will use its internal
820 | #     default value.
821 | #     If you're going to use a stand-alone mail session, you will surely want
822 | #     to configure it, otherwise the internal default value will be used.
823 | #
824 | mail.from = JSPWiki <JSPWiki@localhost>
825 | 
826 | #  B. JNDI Resource Factory Configuration. JSPWiki will try this first.
827 | #     You will need to configure your container to provide a JavaMail
828 | #     resource factory. See your container documentation, or check our
829 | #     fairly complete documentation (with examples for Tomcat) in
830 | #     the JavaDocs for org.apache.wiki.util.MailUtil.
831 | #
832 | #  JNDI resource name. The commented-out value is the default.
833 | #jspwiki.mail.jndiname = mail/Session
834 | 
835 | #  C. Stand-alone Resource Factory. JSPWiki will use these values if JNDI fails.
836 | #
837 | #  Your SMTP host (i.e. the one which sends email)
838 | mail.smtp.host = 127.0.0.1
839 | 
840 | # If for some reason the standard smtp port (25) is blocked, you can change it here
841 | mail.smtp.port = 25
842 | 
843 | # Set what the user sees in the "from" line of the email
844 | #mail.from = JSPWiki <JSPWiki@localhost>
845 | 
846 | # If you are using a webserver that is publicly accessible it usually
847 | # doesn't allow you to send mail anonymously
848 | # (because then this mailserver would become an open relay).
849 | # Therefore you can indicate your account information here...
850 | #
851 | #mail.smtp.account =  your.email@email.com
852 | #mail.smtp.password = yourEmailPassword
853 | 
854 | # The properties below control connection timeouts and TLS (encryption)
855 | # if the mailserver supports it. The commented-out values are the defaults.
